## Bug

A ClawSweeper automerge repair can successfully construct a repaired tree but still fail without publishing it because the nested timeout budgets are too small and incoherent for a large PR.

Observed on openclaw/openclaw#103331:

- Run https://github.com/openclaw/clawsweeper/actions/runs/29107331374 completed its edit pass, then gave the validation-fix worker only about 30 seconds because the enclosing 40-minute step budget was nearly exhausted.
- Retry https://github.com/openclaw/clawsweeper/actions/runs/29109897957 again completed the edit and entered review/fix, but exhausted the enclosing budget before publishing.
- Checkpoint-directed retry https://github.com/openclaw/clawsweeper/actions/runs/29112412294 was treated as a fresh edit and hit the independent 20-minute `CLAWSWEEPER_FIX_CODEX_TIMEOUT_MS` limit.

The workflow currently combines a 20-minute per-Codex-worker limit, a 40-minute fix-step limit, a 5-minute reserve, and a 45-minute execute job. Increasing only one limit cannot fix this.

## Requested fix

Make the repair timeout budget coherent and large enough for bounded large-PR repair:

- default the per-Codex-worker repair timeout to 30 minutes;
- default the enclosing fix execution step to 70 minutes;
- default the execute job to 75 minutes, retaining the 5-minute cleanup/reporting reserve;
- expose the worker and step budgets through repository variables when practical, while preserving safe numeric bounds;
- add tests that assert the budget relationship and prove a later validation/review worker receives meaningful time after a long edit pass;
- update timeout documentation/comments so operators know which values must be increased together.

Keep all existing bounded-attempt behavior and failure reporting. This should be a timeout-budget change, not a relaxation of review, CI, exact-head, or merge gates.
